WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.830
You have just completed a delirium
screen and the result is positive.

2
00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:08.580
Alongside arranging clinical assessment,
you need to decide what to do now.

3
00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.360
What might be contributing?

4
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:12.140
How is the patient feeling?

5
00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.080
What needs to be communicated?

6
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.400
ACE-T brings these early nursing
actions together, so you have a

7
00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:19.640
clear place to start.

8
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.240
The problem is not a shortage
of advice about delirium.

9
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.300
Research papers and clinical protocols
recommend quite different combinations

10
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:31.940
of actions, often without clearly saying
who should carry them out, or when.

11
00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.800
ACE-T was developed to make the initial
nursing contribution more explicit.

12
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.580
It supports a team response
rather than asking nurses

13
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.260
to manage delirium alone.

14
00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:47.320
There are three sections: Acute Triggers,
Patient Experience, and Treatment.

15
00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:52.040
This is a tool for the treatment of
delirium, not a delirium prevention

16
00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:57.260
programme. It follows a positive
detection result; it does not make the

17
00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:01.960
diagnosis. Use it alongside clinical
assessment and your local delirium

18
00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.700
pathway, not as a replacement for either.

19
00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:09.480
The aim is to address the relevant
suggestions within the first four hours

20
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.520
after detection. That gives the
early response a time frame.

21
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:18.460
It does not mean waiting four hours to
act, and it is not a validated safety

22
00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.820
threshold. Escalate urgent
concerns immediately.

23
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.600
The sections can be used
together; they are not a queue.

24
00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.740
Begin by checking observations:
pulse, blood pressure,

25
00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:36.280
oxygen saturation, respiratory rate,
temperature, and conscious level.

26
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.140
Check capillary blood glucose and whether
routine blood tests have been sent.

27
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.060
Escalate abnormal findings as required.

28
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.280
If further investigations are needed,
discuss these with the appropriate

29
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:52.200
clinician, following local policy
and your scope of practice.

30
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.680
Assess for urinary
retention and constipation.

31
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:59.040
Review hydration, nutrition, and pain.

32
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:03.980
Consider infection assessment where
clinically indicated, rather than routine

33
00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.880
urine testing for every
positive delirium screen.

34
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.560
Check whether medication
review has been completed, and

35
00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.960
raise possible medicine-related problems.

36
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.880
The tool helps you bring
together findings, gaps, and

37
00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:18.420
concerns that need action.

38
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.240
Now consider the patient’s experience.

39
00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:26.380
Observe for distress, ask how they
are feeling, and note any agitation.

40
00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.610
The Quick Distress Assessment Tool
may be useful where appropriate.

41
00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.840
Its questions are simple:
“How are you feeling?

42
00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.480
Is anything bothering you?”

43
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.180
A patient does not have to be
agitated to need reassurance

44
00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.560
or help with distress.

45
00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.700
Offer reassurance and reorientation.

46
00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.380
Explain who you are and what is happening.

47
00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:51.460
Check that glasses, hearing aids, and
other communication aids are available.

48
00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:56.200
Consider the environment, including
whether a single room is needed.

49
00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.960
Involve relatives, friends,
or carers where appropriate.

50
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:05.680
These suggestions keep the person’s
experience in view while the team

51
00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:08.060
assesses and treats contributing problems.

52
00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:13.000
For Treatment, consider falls risk
and immediate supportive measures.

53
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:18.100
Fluids or oxygen may be needed,
but neither is an automatic response to

54
00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:23.140
delirium. Document delirium or
suspected delirium, as appropriate,

55
00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.040
and the local screening score.

56
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:29.900
Remember that a positive screening result
requires clinical assessment; it is

57
00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.000
not, on its own, a confirmed diagnosis.

58
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.060
Communicate the score and relevant
findings to the clinical team.

59
00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.380
Inform the family and provide a delirium
leaflet to the patient and family

60
00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:45.340
where appropriate. Discuss the treatment
plan with the multidisciplinary

61
00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:49.920
team. Your nursing care and observations
complement the work of doctors,

62
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.420
advanced practitioners, therapists,
pharmacists, and other colleagues,

63
00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.060
according to local roles.

64
00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:01.520
ACE-T is a set of suggestions, not an
instruction to do every item for every

65
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:06.220
patient. Use your professional judgement
about what is needed, what should be

66
00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.380
added, and what is unnecessary.

67
00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.200
Wording may differ by setting or country.

68
00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:16.600
Relevant actions can be repeated during
ongoing care; treatment does not stop

69
00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.190
when four hours have passed.

70
00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:23.320
The early pilot produced encouraging
staff feedback, not proof of improved

71
00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:28.140
patient outcomes. Think of ACE-T as
a brief prompt for a more explicit

72
00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:32.880
nursing response: look for acute
triggers, attend to the patient’s

73
00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.190
experience, and bring treatment
and communication together.

74
00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:39.700
Take a look with your team,
and consider using it with

75
00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.170
the next suitable patient.
